Z||000002||JohnnyK||06-05-2000||01:49 PM||||Wait.. New idea.. What if I went to a junkyard and cut that piece out of another car.. Has anyone ever tried this anywhere on their frame? Assuming I would have to grind it down a bit to make it fit, but think it would work?||||reg||1
Z||000003||80's BOY||06-05-2000||02:01 PM||||believe it or not, i did the EXACT same procedure on my 85 driver's side. it had been wrecked and bent inward. i just took some clean steel, measured another car and went from there. any mistakes you made in measurements can be corrected by your alignment specialist. i get pretty nervous thinking about those welds sometimes, like when i'm going 100mph!! good luck.||||reg||4
Z||000004||JohnnyK||06-05-2000||04:15 PM||||Where can I look to see if the alignment is smack in the middle? (And if it's not, how can I do that?) Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||80's BOY||06-05-2000||04:27 PM||||i had taken many measurements from all different angles and just welded longer pieces to compensate for the bent part. tack weld first, then re-mount your wheel and eyeball it on a flat surface to see how far off you are. mine as only off 1/4 inch or so for the camber adjustment. maybe explain your situation a little better for a clearer picture of your problem.||||reg||10
Z||000006||JohnnyK||06-05-2000||05:08 PM||||Different situation.. On the rear a-arm, the front mount (of the a-arm) where it attaches to the engine cradle, doesn't anymore because that part of the engine cradle rusted away.. Rest of it looks fine, but there is no support piece in there to mount the a-arm too.. Just a bunch of rust dust now..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> This would be a lot easier if I knew the exact placement of the hole?||||reg||1
